I wanted to like this game, partly because of the review by @Armazati but this is no Star Control or Master of Orion. The lore at first glance appeared intriguing, until you start playing and realize it doesn't hold true, nor does it actually have any depth. I chose the immobile telephatic slaver race for the first playthrough, and even though their lower race should adore them like gods and unquestionably carry out everything, what I see is that there's planet happiness and it's low. I get an event to walk(!!!) to a space cowboy bar and the game dialogue treats me like a human peasant.
Combat sucked, and the laser turrets apparently do not cause damage with the lasers themselves, but by pushing ordnance towards hostiles. How else do you explain them flying donuts around the shots of the laser turrests?!? Furthermore, the range on all weapons is limited, like do you even space? -_-;
That combined to the developer apparently abandoning this project, just avoid this.

It's a nice game with an interesting combination of mechanics. It's tagged as "casual", and I can see how you could play it casually, but it has a lot of depth and a lot of room for optimization. I've enjoyed playing the game so far, and I think it's worth the money, but I also think the game somehow lacks the feeling of achievement... Let me explain;
The game throws at you a growing number of heroes to fight against, and you have a limited capacity to grow in strength, so you definitely lose at some not so far point. It's OK, though, because you're not supposed to "beat" anything at your first game, since you carry things over to your next attempt, so you're significantly more powerful at each iteration. However, regardless of how powerful you get, you'll be overwhelmed regardless. The game doesn't have anything like a checkpoint either, so you don't really feel like you've achieved anything. Was I successful this run? Who knows? Sure, I've killed more heroes, but that's expected since I was expected to get stronger at each iteration...
Anyway, the game is stylistic and fun despite my complaint above.

