is a text-heavy
game (or something). I was excited to play this game for the premise and went in knowing very little about what the game actually consisted in, as I've been understanding it to be the best way to experience short plot-oriented games. There's a total of 4 episodes to go through, each clocking at approximately 1 hour and providing the player a slightly different experience than the last one. Stories Untold isn't very ornate ; it's in how quirky it looks and feels that you'll find its beauty. But don't be fooled by the labels stuck to this game - although definitely a text-heavy game at times reminiscent of the 80's interactive fiction games, there's a lot more to look at than just text. That is, in fact, where this game managed to impress me the most: how it can seamlessly connect a text-based experience to a strong, eerie visual presentation. The thick atmosphere and the pace at which the cryptic plot is explored are some of the strongest assets of the game. Everything worked to keep me on the edge of my seat throughout the better part of the experience.
However, No Code didn't hit the nail in the head in every aspect with this one. As previously implied, you'll spend a significant amount of time wrestling around with enigmas and puzzles, some of which can become a bit tedious. In my opinion this is a small hindrance in the flow of the game as it managed to keep me immersed during most of the time. The only real major issue I have with Stories Untold is how the game has everything in its reach to get us, the players, fully commited and involved in what's going on, as to ultimately reward the player and avoiding dooming the him/her to that one single ending, but ultimately chooses not to and doesn't even acknowledge it. I understand what a linear story is about, but the feeling that you're playing "against" a dumb AI should never be present at a game as linear as this ; you shouldn't be room to think it. If there's a bright side to this, I'd say that there's something magical about a game making you feel desperate to get more involved in the story...
, at least it acknowledge that the player is trying to do it. Thus, No Code missed the mark in terms of ultimately delivering the ultimate psychological thriller. It would also give the game
replay value, which it gravely lacks.
All in all,
is a pretty good psychological visual novel. For those of you wondering, there's no actual horror in this game. The atmosphere is heavy and eerie and there's one or two scarier moments but it's not much, really. At the end of the day, Stories Untold feels like Her Story meets Gone Home... with a dash of Stranger Things, without most of the teenage drama. And I mean all of this in a good way.
7/10

The game is simple, not really unique in its own right, but it has some pretty good elements.
This is one of the best games I have ever played, when it comes to shooting with a handgun. I really like how it feels. It reminds me of Far Cry 4 - I usually ditch all the rifles and just go around with two pistols, cause it's so great!
It has an element, that "if you pay, you will get better stuff", but you can play the game with it being present, since it is not a game breaker.
I am giving it a thumbsup, because the game is free and I can spend an hour or two in here from time to time. But where it has good things going for it, it has major flaws as well.
Pros:
*Good weapon feel (the pistols are amazing for me);
**Different ways you get hurt (general HP loss, bleeding, inability to use two handed weapons);
***Decent working servers;
****Haven't encountered any cheaters so far;
*****Functional modes;
******Decent skill trees and character customisation and development;
*******Good sound;
********Decent optimisation (certainly better than plenty of 15+dollar costing, years in development Early Access offenders).
Cons:
*The anomalies (finally I know what they are ^_^ - portals that generate on the map and disable crucial parts of it) is a useless element to have, often leaving only one path that leads to your interest point. I get the post-apocalyptic setting and all, but they are annoying and really have no purpose. (You can counter them with some higher-end gear, so it might be a way to make us throw some real money to get them, I don't know);
**The maps are unbalanced. Plenty of maps are so one sided, it takes only 1-2 minutes until one team is pushed in to their very spawn points and it is no fun whatsoever (even for the winning team);
***Another game, where grenade throwing is sluggish and takes ages to do;
****No sensible progress have been made to the game since my first visit here and it has been more than a year;
*****Some of the maps are boring throughout - some random spooky elements, sewers and bushes to run through, but they don't tell no story for me and has an uninspired design overall;
******Not many people playing - long'ish wait times (on average 4 minutes - searching in 2/3 regions).
*******Plenty of corners for campers in some maps, campers exploiting them, no limit of snipers and no punishment for just standing still and picking people off - that's not the point of this game.
As I said already, I kill an hour or two in this game from time to time. It is functional, but has plenty of flaws for me to get into it more in-depth, because there is no depth really.

I don't want to sound cliche and use superlatives all the time, but almost everything's just epic about this 2D Platformer:
++ Real life experiences from the Iñupiat/Inuit people are perfectly merged and used in the game's story; this includes culture insights based on video material, which is intriguing, heartwarming & immersive;
++ Another reason to pay attention to your sound configuration: what a great sound effects & soundtrack from
;
++ Personally, I consider the graphics one of the finest I've ever seen for a 2D Platformer. What's even better is, that by far I don't meet the recommended system requirements, but managed to get 60 - 65 FPS in average on max. settings (1440 x 900). My PC may be optimized, but no doubt this game is perfectly optimized as well! Good coding, guys!
+ Nice gameplay with a Single Player and Co-op Mode. Easily switch between characters when alone and still manage to work together while solving some easy, but acceptable puzzles - that aren't the core of the game anyway;
+ A bit too slow-paced sometimes, but it suits the game, that'll 'deliver' you peace of mind if you can identify with the Iñupiat.
I'd say this game is suitable for almost anyone!
9.5/10

