A very long time ago I remember picking up Overlord for forty euro's on launch day at the local game store. How that seems to feel like ages past. I first played Overlord when I was very young, and when I played it I was immediately hooked. I still have the physical retail copy sitting in my closet, still filled with maps of every single dungeon that I myself drew, because I loved this game THAT much. Now that I finally replay it more than seven years later I have absolutely no idea why I loved this game, and that goes to show you that time doesn't wait for anybody.
Overlord is a buggy game, as it has crashed multiple times on my system. Overlord has absolutely abysmal controls. Not because the concept of moving yourself around and controlling minions is difficult, but because the controls are unresponsive and clunky. Sometimes the controls simply would not work: I made the input and nothing happens, often leading to your plan being ruined and causing much frustration.
Overlord is also a game of high promises made shallow and paper-thin. You control a poor man's Sauron through a parody of popular fantasy landscapes in search of a plot. You aren't doing this alone, as you have a small and unintimidating number of brain (and AI)-challenged minions who follow you around and "obey your every command".
While I jokingly said that you were in search of a plot, I was only semi-serious. Your quest is to avenge your predecessor by slaying all seven Heroes who vanquished him, and, while you're at it, re-take over the world. All seven Heroes have become corrupt due to wealth and fame bestowed upon them for succeeding in the quest they undertook. Rhianna Pratchett of all people wrote this game, and while there are occasionally a few "Haha. That's funny. I guess."-moments in the game, most of it honestly feels very forced. It REALLY doesn't help that every single character looks the same, and looking at the lip-synching too long may make you go blind. Seriously, the lip-synching in this game is the stuff of belly-aching hilarity. Or just horror. When anybody talks, it looks as if the game is honestly glitched, of if the animator had a mouth-foaming seizure that day and the janitor had to fill in the blanks.
Graphically the game still looks okay in my book. But be warned, people: 2007 was still in the age of overused bloom and plastic looking textures, though the developers DID try to avoid this in Overlord. The screenshots do a very good job at showing you the actual visuals of how the game is going to look on your screen, minus the UI, of course.
The real problem with this game is that it's overly ambitious to make you feel like an Overlord, a true Dark Lord of Evil, but this promise rings just as hollow as any cave because the Overlord:
-can be easily stunlocked in combat by slugs;
-controls and fights like he has a broomstick up his an*s;
-has an EVIL TOWER OF DOOOOOM that only has four "large" rooms to explore;
-(said tower can also not hold more than one mistress for a reason that's never explained to us);
-can at the very maximum carry 50 weak minions with him;
-has to find the objects that ALLOW him to carry more minions with him, because he starts out with a maximum of five;
-has four types of Minions to choose from, yet out of four, three of them can die almost instantly in combat;
-has a Minion Master that apparantly runs every single decision for him, not making him as an Evil Genius but more of a a Silent Dumb Soldier.
... but I guess "Silent Dumb Soldier" as the title of this game wouldn't have broken the bank in terms of sales numbers (as if there wasn't enough of that back in 2k7). All joking aside, I do hope you guys see my problem here. This is a game that just brings the bare minimum of what is promised and after playing it again it just isn't my idea of what a fantasy evil master would do. That's not to say that this game is worthless though, there IS hard work poured into it, but it's 'One of Those Games' where you have to make your own fun, because the game certainly won't make it for you.
As always, only buy it when it's on sale. Hopefully Overlord III will make some of my evil ambitions come true.
